{#
    An admonition — twelve of them in the core, four tones in this system.

    **The mapping is Sphinx's own grouping, not a ladder of severity.** Sphinx
    splits these into note-like and warning-like, and `important` sits on the
    quiet side of that line: it is emphasis, not a hazard. An author writing
    `.. important::` today means what Sphinx means by it, so a mapping that
    made it an alarm would change what their page says. `tip` is the one type
    that is unambiguously "here is something that helps", which is the tone
    `ok` names.

    Everything unlisted is `info`. That is not a fallback so much as the
    generic form's answer: `.. admonition:: A title of its own` arrives with
    its title slugified into the type name, so the lookup misses by design.

    **No category heading.** Ten of these carry no title at all — the core
    emits `<div class="admonition note"><p>…</p></div>` and nothing else — and
    printing "Note" over each one would be exactly the category name
    `sds-note` forbids its heading to be. The word goes to the glyph, which
    says it out loud without drawing it, and it is the *type's* word rather
    than the tone's: `caution` and `danger` both become `warn`, and a reader
    who cannot see the colour still hears which one this was.

    The title, where there is one, is the author's and it is a label. It goes
    in as text: `heading` is an attribute, so markup inside it would arrive as
    visible angle brackets rather than as emphasis. A title that leans on
    inline markup is a title doing a paragraph's job.
#}
{%- set tone = {
    'tip':        'ok',
    'attention':  'warn',
    'caution':    'warn',
    'warning':    'warn',
    'danger':     'error',
    'error':      'error',
}[name]|default('info') -%}
{%- set said = {
    'note':       'Note',
    'tip':        'Tip',
    'hint':       'Hint',
    'important':  'Important',
    'seealso':    'See also',
    'attention':  'Attention',
    'caution':    'Caution',
    'warning':    'Warning',
    'danger':     'Danger',
    'error':      'Error',
}[name]|default('Note') -%}
{#- Whatever the caller and the source text put on the box, in one attribute.
    Two `class=` on one tag is not two class lists; the browser keeps the
    first and drops the rest. -#}
{%- set marks = [class, node.classes ? node.classesString]|filter(v => v)|join(' ') -%}
{#- The element, not a hand-built box. What an admonition holds is paragraphs,
    lists and whole code blocks, and `sds-note` takes them between its tags for
    exactly this caller. -#}
<sds-note tone="{{ tone }}" label="{{ said }}"
    {%- if title and isTitled %} heading="{{ renderNode(title)|striptags|trim }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if marks %} class="{{ marks }}"{% endif -%}
>{% if title and not isTitled %}<p>{{ renderNode(title) }}</p>{% endif %}{{ renderNode(node) }}</sds-note>
